Added syncing of every IP Address IP Fabric reports for an Interface, rather than one. Secondary addresses, IPv6 addresses and FHRP virtual addresses now reach Nautobot, so a dual-stack Interface or one carrying an HSRP or VRRP address is modelled and can be rendered from Nautobot data. Each address is its own record, matched on its host within its Interface, so one changing is reported and written on its own and one IP Fabric stops reporting is removed without touching the rest; removal goes through Safe Delete Mode, and only where no other Interface holds the address. A Device's `primary_ip4` and `primary_ip6` are set by marking an address already synced from the Interface carrying it, reading IP Fabric's separate `loginIpv4` and `loginIpv6` columns, so a dual-stack Device carries a primary of each version rather than only whichever was read first. Creating an Interface no longer asks the database what addresses it has, which it could not yet have, and the by-Device Interface lookup no longer prefetches addresses no caller reads, so a first sync of a large estate makes materially fewer queries. An address that stops being logged in on is unmarked, so a primary that moves to another address, or a Device IP Fabric reports no login address for, no longer leaves Nautobot pointing at the old one.
